#!/bin/sh
# Runs as root on install and on upgrade of the client package. It changes
# nothing: it says what the `chromium` this package recommends actually needs,
# at the one moment the person installing is looking at a terminal.
set -e

if [ -e /snap/bin/chromium ]; then
    cat <<'NOTE'
Neuman's browser is the Chromium on this machine, which is Ubuntu's snap.

The snap may write only where its own confinement lets it, so Neuman keeps the
profile it opens in ~/snap/chromium/common/neuman-browser rather than under
~/.local. It also has to be allowed to open the DevTools port `browse` uses:

  sudo snap connect chromium:network-bind

Without that the browser starts and `browse` cannot reach it, and the browser
unit will say so. Google Chrome's .deb needs neither step and is what the
Neuman documentation recommends where the snap is not already connected.
NOTE
fi

exit 0
